     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day, double the maximum labeled dose. I know of no clinical setting where that's appropriate. It carries a real risk of serious harm, including lactic acidosis, and a confirmation alone doesn't change that.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** I can do that now, for example 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) or 1,700 mg/day divided twice daily.\n- **Check with pharmacy or the attending.** If there is a reason for an above-maximum dose, a pharmacist can review it and document the rationale. The order can then go through your hospital's override process.\n- **Check the source order.** 1,700 mg may be the intended total daily dose, which was then read as a per-dose amount.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
